{
  "dom_id": "step-q314900",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "Let $f$ be a function such that ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x$ and $f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.$ Use the tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to estimate the value of $f(\\frac{\\pi }{5}).$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$f(\\frac{\\pi }{5})\u2248$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $a=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1,$ and since ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is given by\n\n$$\nL(x)=1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can approximate $f(\\frac{\\pi }{5})$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) & \u2248L(\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}(\\frac{\\pi }{5}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{4}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{30} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{3}}{120}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
